            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Monday Morning Emergency Alert</h2>
<p>When Wang, the performance marketing lead at a utility app's overseas team, opened his computer at 8 AM Monday morning, he found the account dashboard showing "Your advertising account has been disabled." All ad groups turned red, all running campaigns stopped spending, and the business team was pushing for this week's new user acquisition targets.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Among Yiyu Digital Media's clients, over 80% of teams have experienced sudden account suspensions. Most teams respond by repeatedly appealing and waiting, lacking systematic troubleshooting approaches, often resulting in unclear suspension reasons, incomplete appeal materials, and recovery cycles extending beyond two weeks.</p>
<p>Wang's team completed the full cycle from discovery to resolution in 72 hours this time. Here's the complete problem breakdown and action record.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Phase 1: Problem Identification (0-4 hours)</h2>
<h3>What Was Discovered</h3>
<p>After logging into the account backend, Wang found Meta's official notice on the "Account Quality" page:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Your advertising account has been disabled for violating advertising policies. Violation type: Misleading claims."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But the notice didn't specify which particular ad or creative violated the policy.</p>
<h3>Decision Criteria</h3>
<p>In Yiyu Digital Media's compliance audit SOP, common reasons for account suspension fall into four categories:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Typical Symptoms</th>
<th>Urgency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Creative violations</td>
<td>Images/videos with false elements, exaggerated claims</td>
<td>High</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Landing page violations</td>
<td>Page content doesn't match ad promises</td>
<td>High</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Domain risk</td>
<td>Website flagged by security platforms</td>
<td>Medium</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Abnormal account behavior</td>
<td>Frequent modifications in short term, audience fluctuation</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Wang's team first checked ads modified in the past 48 hours and discovered that a batch of A/B testing creatives launched Sunday night included an ad with the text "Best Free App Ever" — an unprovable superlative claim that falls under typical misleading content in Meta's policies.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Creative violation is the primary cause; appeal materials need to be prepared.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Phase 2: Evidence Collection and Appeal Preparation (4-24 hours)</h2>
<h3>Quick Checklist</h3>
<p>The team verified items one by one according to the following checklist:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] New/modified ad creatives in the past 7 days</li>
<li>[ ] Consistency between landing pages and ad promises</li>
<li>[ ] Whether conversion tracking code triggered abnormal events</li>
<li>[ ] Whether audience settings contained discriminatory exclusions</li>
<li>[ ] Domain safety scores on VirusTotal, VirusDB</li>
<li>[ ] Compliance requirements in target countries (GDPR, CCPA)</li>
</ul>
<p>After completing the investigation, Wang's team confirmed only one obvious violation: a video ad's cover used before-and-after comparison screenshots, but the comparison effect was excessively rendered by AI, constituting "misleading claims."</p>
<h3>Appeal Material Preparation</h3>
<p>Yiyu Digital Media's compliance SOP recommends submitting three types of materials for appeals:</p>
<p><strong>1. Acknowledgment and Explanation</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>"We confirm that the ad used before-and-after comparison effects that were not adequately verified, violating Meta's advertising policies. We commit to immediately pausing this ad and permanently removing the related content."</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>2. Rectification Evidence</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Screenshot of deleted violating ad</li>
<li>Draft of new ad (using conservative language: "helps improve" instead of "significantly increases")</li>
<li>Landing page content review records</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>3. Account History</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Spending and conversion data from the past 90 days</li>
<li>No major violations history for this account</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Phase 3: Submitting Appeal and Follow-up (24-48 hours)</h2>
<h3>Appeal Channel Selection</h3>
<p>There are three channels for Meta ad account appeals. SOP recommended priority order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Business Customer Support</strong> (Recommended): Submit through account manager; response usually within 24-48 hours</li>
<li><strong>Ad Policy Support Form</strong>: Suitable for small and medium accounts without dedicated account managers</li>
<li><strong>Community Forum Appeal</strong>: Informal channel; results vary</li>
</ol>
<p>Wang's team contacted Yiyu Digital Media's business liaison and submitted the appeal package through the enterprise customer support channel.</p>
<h3>Progress After 24 Hours</h3>
<p>Tuesday morning, they received Meta's email reply:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"We have completed our review. Your account will be restored within 24 hours. During this period, please ensure all running ads comply with our advertising policies."</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Phase 4: Review and Prevention Mechanism (48-72 hours)</h2>
<h3>Post-Recovery Actions</h3>
<p>Wednesday morning, the account status returned to normal. But Wang didn't stop there — they needed to prevent similar issues from happening again.</p>
<p>The team executed Yiyu Digital Media compliance SOP's prevention module:</p>
<p><strong>a) Pre-launch Creative Review</strong><br />
Established a dual-review mechanism: Media buyer submits creative → Compliance lead reviews copy → Test account trial → Official launch. This adds 4-6 hours buffer but significantly reduces suspension risk.</p>
<p><strong>b) Keyword Blocklist Library</strong><br />
Compiled a "prohibited expressions list":</p>
<ul>
<li>Absolute superlatives: "best ever" "#1" "100%"</li>
<li>Effect promises: "guaranteed" "cure" "prevent"</li>
<li>Competitor comparisons: "better than" "beats"</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>c) Account Health Monitoring</strong><br />
Set up daily review tasks: Check Ad Policy Center notifications, review account quality scores, monitor abnormal spending fluctuations.</p>
<hr />
